The dc_clos_bgp fabric is currently healthy. All five FRR routing stacks (both
leaf routers, both spine routers, and the super-spine) started fresh at 15:27
today — the log files contain no entries predating that timestamp, even though
the Docker containers themselves have been running for over a day. This is the
only anomalous artefact: the FRR processes were absent or had crashed across
all devices until 15:27, meaning the fabric had no routing plane during whatever
window the user experienced the outage. At 15:27 the FRR stack was restarted
(whether manually by the infra team or by some other mechanism), BGP sessions
re-established within roughly two minutes, and full connectivity was restored
before this investigation began.

At investigation time (15:35+), end-to-end data-plane reachability between
pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) and pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) is confirmed: 4/4 ICMP pings with
0% loss, traceroute shows the correct four-hop path through leaf_router_0_0 →
spine_router_0_0 → leaf_router_0_1 → pc_0_1, and nc confirms TCP packets
reach the destination (RST returned, no service listening — which is expected).
ip_forward=1 and empty iptables on all routers rule out any data-plane filter.
All BGP sessions are Established, route tables are correct on every device, and
all interfaces are UP with MTU 1500.

The infra team's narrative that "one spine box died" is not supported by the
evidence: both spine routers have identical, symmetrical configurations and
healthy BGP state. No corrective action is needed for the current network state.
The outage the user experienced was a transient routing-plane blackout that
self-resolved when FRR was restarted at 15:27.
